        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:</p><p><span>Spaceflight associated neuro-ocular syndrome (SANS) is a top human spaceflight risk, for which NASA does not yet have a proven mitigation strategy. Thigh cuffs (Braslet) and lower-body negative pressure (LBNP; Chibis) devices have been evaluated as a means to reduce SANS signs and symptoms, but these methods alone may not provide sufficient relief of cephalic venous congestion and prevent SANS symptoms. Additionally, current LBNP devices are large and do not allow crewmembers to move freely about the cabin while in use, limiting their ability to be used routinely as a countermeasure. Therefore, a novel countermeasure is needed that is easy to implement and provides specific relief of symptoms. </span></p><p><span><span>This investigation evaluated the impedance threshold device (ITD) as a SANS countermeasure. The ITD provides up to 7 cm H2O (~5 mm Hg) resistance to inspiratory airflow, lowering the intrathoracic pressure (ITP) and effectively augmenting the function of thorax as a vacuum pump during inspiration. The ITD was developed to augment venous return to the central circulation and increase cardiac output during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) or in patients with hypotension, and is approved by the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for that purpose. While the effect of an ITD on CPR survival outcomes is controversial, the ITD’s ability to lower ITP with a concomitant decrease in intracranial pressure (ICP) is well documented. The investigators  hypothesized that ITD breathing (similar to repeated Mueller maneuvers) in supine or head-down subjects would reduce ITP during the respiratory cycle, serving to reduce venous congestion in the neck and lower ICP. </span></span></p><ol>
</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:</p><p><span>

Fifteen healthy test subjects each participated in two trials, one with an ITD and one with a sham ITD (placebo), in multiple postures: seated, supine, 6° head-down tilt (HDT), 15° HDT, and extended 15° HDT (additional 30 minutes). Measures analyzed from each posture included internal jugular vein (IJV) cross-sectional area (CSA), noninvasive IJV pressure (VeinPress), superior ophthalmic vein direction of flow, choroid thickness (using Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT)), central retinal artery (CRA) and vein (CRV) Doppler, noninvasive estimates of ICP (cochlear and cerebral fluid pressure analyzer and otoacoustic emissions), intraocular pressure (IOP), arterial blood pressure, and heart rate. Questionnaires were administered at each posture to document subjective status. 
</span><br></p>
<p>RESULTS:</p><p><span>All sham postural profiles demonstrated expected trends (e.g., parameters such as IJV CSA, noninvasive IJV pressure, and ICP estimates all increased with increasing HDT). IJV CSA and JV pressures were decreased with ITD in most postures, supporting the hypothesis that ITD facilitates drainage of the head and neck. CRA and CRV velocities increased with increasing HDT, but were decreased with ITD. The most logical explanation for this response is the reduction in vessel diameter during HDT, with a re-expansion induced by ITD. Constraint of the CRA and CRV is mediated by the optic nerve and sheath, however is not necessarily due to ICP alone; autoregulation and pressures in the intraconal space likely play a significant role. </span></p><p><span>

Choroid thickness, IOP, and noninvasive ICP estimates did not show systematic difference between ITD and sham. Lack of ICP reduction with ITD was unexpected given ample documentation of this effect in the literature. It is possible that our ear-based non-invasive ICP methods were particularly sensitive to the altered airway pressure induced by ITD, masking any changes in ICP. In similar ground-based experiments, choroid thickness was also not reduced with LBNP. </span></p><p><span>

In the context of the posture-based model that was employed,&nbsp;the conclusions are: 1) based on IJV parameters, ITD unequivocally relieves congestion in (filling of) the large neck veins; 2) based on ocular Doppler data, ITD has favorable peripheral vascular effects in the orbit (i.e., in the direction opposite to those demonstrated in simulated or actual microgravity), and 3)&nbsp;the data do not demonstrate a clear reduction in ICP with ITD. 
</span><br>&nbsp;
</p>
